INSERT INTO blog_post_translations
  (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, body_html, seo_title, seo_description)
VALUES (
  :post_id, 'en', $x$buy-in-game-currency-safely$x$,
  $x$How to buy in-game currency safely$x$,
  $x$How to spot scam sellers, check a service in a minute, protect your card details, and why a top-up by player ID is safer than handing over a password.$x$,
$x$<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Safe buying starts with checking the seller: domain, contacts, reviews and payment method matter more than a low price.</li>
<li>Automatic delivery removes the main risk of manual schemes: nobody can take the money and vanish, or ask for your account.</li>
<li>Never give a currency seller your game login and password: legitimate top-ups go by player ID.</li>
<li>When paying by card, check HTTPS, 3-D Secure confirmation, and that nothing beyond card details is asked for.</li>
</ul>
<p>Safe buying rests on three checks: you buy from a service with clear contacts, you pay through a protected payment page, and you receive the top-up by player ID rather than handing over a login and password. If any of the three fails, cancel the purchase, however good the price looks.</p>
<h2>Why it deserves attention</h2>
<p>Gaming platforms are a favourite target. Kaspersky counted more than 20 million attacks related to gaming platforms in January–October 2025 (<a href="https://www.kaspersky.com/about/press-releases/kaspersky-reports-64-million-shopping-phishing-attempts-and-over-20-million-gaming-attacks-detected-in-2025">Kaspersky</a>). For a gamer that means one thing: the page where you type card details for a quick top-up may well be fake.</p>
<h2>Signs of a scam site or seller</h2>
<ul>
<li>A price far below the market. UC for PUBG Mobile or Genesis Crystals several times cheaper than anywhere else is bait, not a bargain.</li>
<li>Payment only to a personal card or in crypto. An honest service takes money through payment providers, not as a transfer &quot;to a friend&quot;.</li>
<li>A request for your login and password. Legitimate top-ups go by player ID; account access is what a scammer needs to hijack it or strip valuable items.</li>
<li>No contacts or company details. No name, no working support, no public reviews means nobody is accountable.</li>
<li>Pressure and haste. &quot;Today only&quot;, &quot;two left&quot;, &quot;pay now&quot;: manufactured urgency stops you checking.</li>
<li>Typos, a fresh domain, broken layout, no HTTPS: a phishing page built in a hurry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Player-to-player resale marketplaces carry their own risk: there is no guarantor, and you are alone with a seller you have never seen.</p>
<h2>Why automation matters</h2>
<p>The weak point of manual schemes is the person between your payment and your top-up. They can take the money and disappear, drag delivery out for a day, or demand more. Automation removes that link.</p>
<p>In an automated service you enter the player ID, pay, and the currency is credited at once, with no operator. That is how YuPay works: enter the ID, press &quot;Check&quot; to see the account was found, pay with Click, Payme or Uzum, and the top-up arrives automatically. The total in soʻm is shown before payment and exactly that is charged.</p>
<p>Automation also solves the second problem: you never hand over a login and password. The top-up is tied to a public identifier, and access to the account stays with you. More on how it works in <a href="/en/blog/online-game-top-ups-uzbekistan">online game top-ups</a>.</p>
<h2>How to check a service</h2>
<ul>
<li>Domain and connection: HTTPS, no browser warnings, spelling checked letter by letter.</li>
<li>Contacts: a name, working support, public terms.</li>
<li>Payment methods: local methods and prices in soʻm through payment providers beat a transfer to a personal card.</li>
<li>Delivery mechanics: a top-up should need the player ID, not a login and password. This is the key sign of a legitimate service.</li>
<li>Reviews and history: look for recent reviews on external sites, not only quotes on the site itself.</li>
</ul>
<p>Why buying from a local seller is often both cheaper and safer than a foreign middleman is covered in <a href="/en/blog/why-buy-game-top-ups-locally">why it pays to buy game top-ups locally</a>.</p>
<h2>Protecting card details</h2>
<ul>
<li>The payment page should belong to the bank or payment system, not be a home-made field on the shop's page.</li>
<li>3-D Secure confirmation with a one-time code protects the card from being used without you.</li>
<li>Enter only payment details: card number, expiry and CVC. PIN, online-banking password and the codes the bank tells you never to share go to nobody.</li>
<li>Check the address right before typing: phishing copies the look of known sites.</li>
<li>Use a card with limits and notifications, so damage is contained even if details leak.</li>
</ul>
<p>The PCI DSS standard requires payment services to protect the payment page from card-stealing scripts. For a buyer that is an argument for services that pay through a trusted provider rather than collecting card details themselves.</p>
<h2>The checklist</h2>
<p>Run it before every payment; if one item fails, stop.</p>
<ul>
<li>HTTPS, domain spelled right.</li>
<li>A name, contacts and working support.</li>
<li>A market price, nothing &quot;too good to be true&quot;.</li>
<li>A player ID is enough; no login or password.</li>
<li>Payment through a provider, not to a personal card.</li>
<li>A protected payment page with 3-D Secure.</li>
<li>Only card number, expiry and CVC entered.</li>
<li>Notifications on, ideally a limit on online payments.</li>
</ul>
<p>Safety comes almost entirely from choosing the service and paying carefully. Pick one trusted service with automatic delivery and payment in soʻm, bookmark it, and buy only there.</p>$x$,
  $x$How to buy in-game currency safely$x$,
  $x$Buying game currency safely: the signs of a scam seller, how to check a service in a minute, protecting card details, and why a top-up by ID beats sharing a password.$x$
);

